C. CREATE A DOCKERFILE FOR A MAVEN EXECUTABLE JAR PROJECT
---------------------------------------------------------
From the project root (the folder containing pom.xml), create a file named exactly: Dockerfile

Example multi-stage Dockerfile for a Java 17 Maven application that creates an executable JAR:

----- BEGIN Dockerfile (JAR) -----
FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY pom.xml .
COPY src ./src
RUN mvn -B clean package -DskipTests

FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app/target/*.jar app.jar
EXPOSE 8080
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
----- END Dockerfile (JAR) -----

IMPORTANT:
- This example expects an executable JAR (for example, a Spring Boot JAR).
- Change Java 17 to the version required by pom.xml if necessary.
- If the project creates a WAR, use the WAR example below instead.

D. ALTERNATIVE DOCKERFILE FOR A TOMCAT WAR PROJECT
--------------------------------------------------
Create Dockerfile in the project root:

----- BEGIN Dockerfile (WAR) -----
FROM tomcat:9.0
RUN rm -rf /usr/local/tomcat/webapps/*
COPY target/*.war /usr/local/tomcat/webapps/ROOT.war
EXPOSE 8080
CMD ["catalina.sh", "run"]
----- END Dockerfile (WAR) -----

Use a Tomcat version compatible with the project's Servlet API. A legacy javax.servlet application commonly requires Tomcat 9; Jakarta-based applications may require Tomcat 10 or later.

