4. DOCKER COMPOSE - WEB APPLICATION + MYSQL
============================================================

A. CREATE PROJECT FOLDER AND FILE
---------------------------------
mkdir SE-Compose
cd SE-Compose

Create a file named docker-compose.yml (or compose.yaml). Paste the example below.

B. EXAMPLE docker-compose.yml
-----------------------------
This example starts a Docker Hub web image and a MySQL database. Replace the web image with the image required by the practical. The web application must be programmed/configured to read the DB_* environment variables shown here.

----- BEGIN docker-compose.yml -----
services:
  web:
    image: <DOCKERHUB_USERNAME>/<WEB_IMAGE>:latest
    container_name: web-app
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
    environment:
      DB_HOST: db
      DB_PORT: "3306"
      DB_NAME: college_db
      DB_USER: appuser
      DB_PASSWORD: app123
    depends_on:
      db:
        condition: service_healthy

  db:
    image: mysql:8.0
    container_name: mysql-db
    restart: unless-stopped
    environment:
      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: root123
      MYSQL_DATABASE: college_db
      MYSQL_USER: appuser
      MYSQL_PASSWORD: app123
    ports:
      - "3306:3306"
    volumes:
      - mysql_data:/var/lib/mysql
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "mysqladmin ping -h localhost -uroot -p$${MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD} --silent"]
      interval: 10s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 5

volumes:
  mysql_data:
----- END docker-compose.yml -----

IMPORTANT NOTES:
- Replace <DOCKERHUB_USERNAME>/<WEB_IMAGE> with a real image name. Do not leave angle brackets in the YAML.
- The application connects to the database host "db", not "localhost". Compose service names work as DNS names on the Compose network.
- The DB_* environment variable names are examples. The actual web image must support those names or use the variable names documented by its developer.
- This is a lab example. Use strong secrets and avoid exposing MySQL port 3306 publicly in real deployments.

