6. NAGIOS CORE - INSTALL AND MONITOR ON UBUNTU
============================================================

A. INSTALL NAGIOS CORE PACKAGE
------------------------------
The following is the Ubuntu package-based route. Package setup and Apache configuration can vary slightly by Ubuntu release.

sudo apt update
sudo apt install nagios4 nagios-nrpe-plugin

During installation, follow package prompts and set/remember the Nagios web password if requested.

B. CREATE / SET UP NAGIOS WEB USER
----------------------------------
Ubuntu's documented Nagios 4 package uses Digest authentication:
sudo htdigest -c /etc/nagios4/htdigest.users "Nagios4" nagiosadmin

Use -c only when creating the password file for the first time. Do not use -c when adding another user to an existing file.

C. ENABLE APACHE CONFIGURATION
------------------------------
sudo cp /etc/nagios4/apache2.conf /etc/apache2/sites-available/nagios4.conf
sudo a2ensite nagios4.conf
sudo systemctl reload apache2
sudo systemctl reload nagios4

D. VERIFY SERVICES AND CONFIGURATION
------------------------------------
sudo systemctl status nagios4
sudo systemctl status apache2
sudo nagios4 -v /etc/nagios4/nagios.cfg

If configuration validation reports errors, fix them before restarting:
sudo systemctl restart nagios4
sudo systemctl restart apache2

E. OPEN NAGIOS WEB INTERFACE
----------------------------
On the same machine:
http://localhost/nagios4

From another machine:
http://<NAGIOS_SERVER_IP>/nagios4

If using UFW and remote HTTP access is required:
sudo ufw allow Apache
sudo ufw status

Also allow TCP port 80 in the cloud/network firewall if Nagios runs on EC2.

F. IMPORTANT NAGIOS PATHS
-------------------------
Main configuration:       /etc/nagios4/nagios.cfg
Custom configuration:     /etc/nagios4/conf.d/
Plugin configuration:     /etc/nagios-plugins/config/
Remote NRPE configuration: /etc/nagios/nrpe.cfg (on monitored host)
Apache configuration:     /etc/nagios4/apache2.conf
Nagios CGI permissions:   /etc/nagios4/cgi.cfg

G. SERVICE CONTROL COMMANDS
---------------------------
sudo systemctl start nagios4
sudo systemctl stop nagios4
sudo systemctl restart nagios4
sudo systemctl reload nagios4
sudo systemctl status nagios4

sudo systemctl start apache2
sudo systemctl restart apache2
sudo systemctl status apache2

H. COMMON NAGIOS PLUGIN CHECKS
------------------------------
List plugins:
ls /usr/lib/nagios/plugins/

Check plugin help:
/usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_ping -h
/usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_http -h
/usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_disk -h

Example manual checks:
/usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_ping -H 127.0.0.1 -w 100.0,20% -c 500.0,60%
/usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_http -H localhost
/usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_disk -w 20% -c 10% -p /

I. ADD A BASIC CUSTOM HOST (CHECK INCLUDED CONFIGURATION FIRST)
---------------------------------------------------------------
1. Confirm that /etc/nagios4/nagios.cfg includes the custom directory:
   grep -n "conf.d" /etc/nagios4/nagios.cfg

2. Create a configuration file:
   sudo nano /etc/nagios4/conf.d/lab-host.cfg

3. Example host definition (generic-host template must exist in your installed configuration):

define host {
    use         generic-host
    host_name   lab-ubuntu
    alias       Lab Ubuntu Server
    address     <TARGET_SERVER_IP>
}

4. Validate, then restart:
   sudo nagios4 -v /etc/nagios4/nagios.cfg
   sudo systemctl restart nagios4

J. OPTIONAL: INSTALL NRPE ON A REMOTE UBUNTU MACHINE
---------------------------------------------------
On the REMOTE machine being monitored:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install nagios-nrpe-server
sudo systemctl enable --now nagios-nrpe-server
sudo systemctl status nagios-nrpe-server

Edit NRPE configuration if required:
sudo nano /etc/nagios/nrpe.cfg

Set allowed_hosts to include localhost and the Nagios server IP, following the file's existing syntax. Then:
sudo systemctl restart nagios-nrpe-server

On Nagios server, test NRPE connectivity (after network access is allowed):
/usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_nrpe -H <REMOTE_SERVER_IP>

Nagios/Nrpe deployments require correct firewall rules and matching allowed_hosts settings. Do not expose NRPE port 5666 to the entire internet.

