8. AWS EC2 - LAUNCH, SSH, UBUNTU AND DEPLOYMENT
============================================================

A. LAUNCH EC2 FROM AWS CONSOLE
------------------------------
1. Open https://console.aws.amazon.com/ec2/
2. Choose the required AWS Region.
3. Select Launch instance.
4. Enter an instance name.
5. Select the required AMI (for example, Ubuntu Server).
6. Select an instance type allowed by the lab/account.
7. Create or select a key pair and download the .pem file.
8. Configure a Security Group.
9. Launch the instance and wait until it is Running and status checks pass.
10. Copy the Public IPv4 address.

B. COMMON EC2 SECURITY GROUP INBOUND PORTS
------------------------------------------
SSH        TCP 22     Source: Your administrator IP (/32)
HTTP       TCP 80     Source: As required for public web access
HTTPS      TCP 443    Source: As required for public HTTPS access
Tomcat     TCP 8080   Source: Restrict to lab/client IP where possible
Nagios     TCP 80     Source: Restrict to required users/network

Do not expose MySQL TCP 3306 publicly. Add only the ports needed for the practical.

C. CONNECT TO UBUNTU EC2 USING SSH
----------------------------------
From Linux/macOS/WSL terminal, in the folder containing your key:
chmod 400 <KEY_NAME>.pem
ssh -i "<KEY_NAME>.pem" ubuntu@<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>

Windows PowerShell example:
ssh -i .\<KEY_NAME>.pem ubuntu@<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>

If Windows reports that the private key permissions are too open, restrict the key file ACL (run in PowerShell from the key folder):
icacls .\<KEY_NAME>.pem /inheritance:r
icacls .\<KEY_NAME>.pem /grant:r "$($env:USERNAME):(R)"

If the AMI is Amazon Linux, the SSH username is commonly ec2-user, not ubuntu.

