sudo systemctl restart <SERVICE>
sudo systemctl enable <SERVICE>
sudo systemctl disable <SERVICE>
sudo journalctl -u <SERVICE> -n 100 --no-pager

H. FIREWALL COMMANDS (UFW)
--------------------------
sudo ufw status
sudo ufw allow 22/tcp
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp
sudo ufw allow 8080/tcp
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw reload

Before enabling UFW on a remote EC2 server, allow SSH port 22 first or you may lock yourself out. AWS Security Groups must also permit the traffic.

I. CLONE AND BUILD MAVEN WEB APPLICATION ON EC2
-----------------------------------------------
git clone <GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL>
cd <PROJECT_FOLDER>
git status
mvn clean test package
ls -lh target/

J. TOMCAT INSTALLATION - CHECK AVAILABLE VERSION FIRST
------------------------------------------------------
Tomcat package name and version vary with Ubuntu release. Search available versions:
apt-cache search '^tomcat[0-9]'

Install the version compatible with the application's Servlet/Jakarta API. Examples (use the version available for your OS):
sudo apt install tomcat10

For a legacy project that specifically requires Tomcat 9, use a compatible Tomcat 9 distribution/package if available rather than deploying it blindly to Tomcat 10+.

Check service names after installation:
systemctl list-unit-files | grep -i tomcat

Example for a Tomcat 10 package:
sudo systemctl enable --now tomcat10
sudo systemctl status tomcat10

Common packaged webapps directory for Tomcat 10:
/var/lib/tomcat10/webapps/

For a Tomcat 9 package, the directory is commonly:
/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/

K. DEPLOY WAR TO TOMCAT
-----------------------
First build the WAR:
mvn clean package
ls target/*.war

Example for Tomcat 10 package (adjust version/path to actual installation):
sudo cp target/<APP_NAME>.war /var/lib/tomcat10/webapps/
sudo systemctl restart tomcat10

Example for Tomcat 9 package:
sudo cp target/<APP_NAME>.war /var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/
sudo systemctl restart tomcat9

Check service status and logs:
sudo systemctl status tomcat10
sudo journalctl -u tomcat10 -n 100 --no-pager

Access from browser, using actual WAR context path:
http://<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>:8080/<APP_NAME>/

If the WAR was deployed as ROOT.war, use:
http://<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>:8080/

Ensure the EC2 Security Group allows TCP 8080 and the application is listening. If Tomcat is behind port 80 reverse proxy, configure that separately.

L. COPY FILE FROM YOUR LOCAL PC TO EC2 (SCP)
--------------------------------------------
Run on your LOCAL computer, not inside EC2:
scp -i <KEY_NAME>.pem target/<APP_NAME>.war ubuntu@<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>:/tmp/

Then SSH into EC2 and copy the WAR into the correct Tomcat webapps folder.

M. CHECK PUBLIC ACCESS
----------------------
On EC2:
curl -I http://localhost:8080/<APP_CONTEXT>/

From your own browser:
http://<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>:8080/<APP_CONTEXT>/

Check these if not accessible:
1. EC2 instance is running and has a Public IPv4 address.
2. Security Group allows inbound TCP 8080 (or the port used).
3. Application/Tomcat service is active.
4. Application is listening on the expected port.
5. Subnet route table and Internet Gateway permit internet access.
6. OS firewall is not blocking the port.

N. STOP / START / TERMINATE EC2
-------------------------------
Use AWS Console or AWS CLI if configured.
Stop: powers off the instance; EBS-backed data is generally retained.
Start: starts the stopped instance; public IPv4 may change unless using Elastic IP.
Terminate: deletes the instance; EBS volumes configured for deletion on termination may also be deleted.

